far and the and the potential for the future ones to do so and the mayor did request they reconsider the forth closure and of course I will go on with the next slide from their perspective that does not fit their model from the BNSF perspective their primary considerations overall are the safety operation and efficiency and maintenance of of their railroad um that is their core priority and from their perspective redevelopment may restrict future industrial use of the corridor whereas in our perspective redevelopment really is that economic catalyst so again you see two different sides of the story both trying to get to a different means and from again from their perspective when you increase pedestrians and

vehicles in any manner it presents a safety and operational challenge for them and that redevelopment introduces that from there from there it's complete exposure to liability and there in lies the difference between the two perspectives so the position of the road closures obviously as I've discussed before BNSFU's road closure measures as the as the only method for improving safety and reducing liability from their standpoint while still permitting us to redevelop in the corridor so again just two different sides of the fence um but they want us to allow us to move forward but the exchange is we have to get these road closures for them to be able to limit their liability so from the mutual benefit side for both of us when we upgrade the crossings and

enhance we really enhance the overall safety of both the corridor users and the railroad operations those drainage improvements then also reduce flooding which improves safety from their standpoint and and reliability for them and then also for us and then finally this is the point that we finally got to at the end of the meeting which was a gamecher in my mind basically they understood then if we we were talking about having no pedestrian access on the west side and what the potential would be is we introduced people trying to bypass jump over the fences at those areas that we closed the crossing because remember a crossing is both for vehicles and pedestrians so as it as they began to look at it they realized that we did need pedestrian connectivity on the west side so

